orthant 1 release

= [Bit flags](flags.md) holds a set of an enumeration's cases in a single word.
=
+ A map with many entries is quicker to fill in place than to grow one `define` at a time.
+ `Map.build` lends a block a builder to write to, and gives back an ordinary `Map` when the
+ block ends:
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ val court = Map.build[Text, Int]: map =>
+   map(t"Lear") = 80
+   map(t"Kent") = 48
+   map(t"Gloucester") = 60
+
+ Out.println(court.at(t"Kent").inspect)
+ Out.println((court == ages.define(t"Gloucester", 60)).inspect)
+ ```
+
+ Only the builder can write to the map, and the builder cannot leave its block: keeping a
+ reference to it for later is a compile error. So the map is immutable from the moment `build`
+ returns, and equal to any other map with the same entries, however that one was made.
+
+ ### Maps with keys known in advance
+
+ A map whose keys are all written where it is made can be read faster still. `Map.fixed` finds a
+ *perfect hash* of its keys while the code is compiled: a small calculation which sends each key
+ to a slot of its own. A read is then that calculation and one comparison, whether or not the
+ key is there:
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ val cast = Map.fixed(t"Lear" -> 80, t"Kent" -> 48, t"Gloucester" -> 60, t"Edgar" -> 28)
+
+ Out.println(cast.at(t"Edgar").inspect)
+ Out.println(cast.at(t"Fool").inspect)
+ Out.println((cast == court.define(t"Edgar", 28)).inspect)
+ ```
+
+ The keys must be literals: texts, `Int`s, `Long`s or `Char`s, all of one kind, and each given
+ once. The values can be any expressions. A fixed map is an ordinary `Map` to everything which
+ uses it, and `define` and `omit` work on it as on any other.
+
+ A map which cannot be fixed does not compile, so a map made with `fixed` is never a slower one
+ in disguise. Giving a key twice is one such error:
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ compile
+ errors show
+ ##
+ Map.fixed(t"Lear" -> 80, t"Kent" -> 48, t"Lear" -> 81)
+ ```
+
+ A key which is not a literal is another, as are keys of different kinds, and two texts which
+ happen to share a hash code. A map of more than 1,024 entries is too large to write out this
+ way, and is better filled with `Map.build`.
+
+ When the keys are only known once the program is running, `optimize` gives an existing map the
+ same treatment. It finds the hash then, and compiles it with the Scala compiler:
+
+ ```scala
+ import murmuration.optimize
+ ```
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ typecheck
+ ##
+ val quick = court.optimize
+ ```
+
+ The compiler must therefore be on the classpath when the program runs, which is why `optimize`
+ comes from a module of its own, in the `staged` bundle, and only on the JVM. Compiling takes
+ about a tenth of a second for each map, and several seconds for the first, so it suits a map
+ which is made once and read a great many times.
+
+ It helps a map keyed by numbers most, which reads about twice as fast. A map keyed by text
+ reads faster when its keys are usually found, and slower when they usually are not, since a
+ text which is absent must still be compared with the one in its slot. A map of four entries or
+ fewer, or one whose keys are not all texts, all `Int`s, all `Long`s or all `Char`s, is given
+ back as it was.
+
